Chapter: GopEnum
  cites Works chapter GopDisk
  cites Works chapter GopAhci
  cites Works chapter GopNvme
  cites Works chapter GopUsbMsc
  cites Works chapter GopFat16
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The disk inventory. GopMedium answers one question in private -- which
 medium is mine -- and locks the answer into GopDisk's selection cells.
 This chapter answers the wider question out loud: what disks does this
 machine have at all? Every AHCI port, the NVMe namespace, the legacy
 IDE drive, the USB stick -- each named by its own IDENTIFY or INQUIRY
 data, sized in sectors, and probed for an EFI System Partition, its
 volume label, and our own CODEX.CDX. The demo this exists for: the
 boot stick lists every disk in the machine by name and points at
 itself.

 Enumeration rides the same seam the medium probe rides: steering the
 selection cells at a candidate makes the whole FAT stack above read
 from it. Unlike the probe, enumeration must leave no trace -- the
 cells are saved before the walk and restored after it, magic written
 last, so a locked boot medium stays locked and a cleared machine
 stays cleared no matter what the walk touched.

 We say:

Section: Inventory Record

  DiskInfo = record {
    di-kind : Integer,
    di-index : Integer,
    di-model : Text,
    di-sectors : Integer,
    di-label : Text,
    di-codex : Boolean
  }

  enum-kind-name : Integer -> Text
  enum-kind-name (k) =
    if k == med-kind-ahci then "AHCI"
    else if k == med-kind-ide then "IDE"
    else if k == med-kind-usb then "USB"
    else if k == med-kind-nvme then "NVMe"
    else "?"

  enum-size-mb : Integer -> Integer
  enum-size-mb (sectors) = sectors / 2048

Section: ASCII Decoding

 Identify pages carry ASCII, space-padded, and on a device that never
 filled the field, zeros or garbage. The decode stops at the first
 byte outside the printable range and then trims trailing spaces, so
 a blank or hostile field becomes the empty text instead of a smear
 of gibberish on the boot screen. The bytes go through gfat-text,
 whose from-unicode step is the ASCII-to-CCE conversion every disk
 string needs.

  enum-printable-len : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  enum-printable-len (buf) (off) (len) (i) =
    if i >= len then i
    else let b = peek-byte buf (off + i)
    in if b < 32 then i
    else if b > 126 then i
    else enum-printable-len buf off len (i + 1)

  enum-trim-len : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  enum-trim-len (buf) (off) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then 0
    else if peek-byte buf (off + n - 1) == 32 then enum-trim-len buf off (n - 1)
    else n

  enum-ascii : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Text
  enum-ascii (buf) (off) (len) =
    let p = enum-printable-len buf off len 0
    in let n = enum-trim-len buf off p
    in gfat-text buf off (off + n) ""

Section: ATA Identify Decoding

 ATA strings are packed two characters to a 16-bit word with the first
 character in the HIGH byte, and the words land little-endian in the
 byte stream -- so consecutive characters arrive pairwise swapped. The
 decoder unswaps into a scratch buffer and reads it as plain ASCII.
 The model occupies words 27-46: forty characters at byte offset 54.

  enum-ata-unswap : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  enum-ata-unswap (src) (off) (dst) (i) (words) =
    if i >= words then 0
    else let c0 = peek-byte src (off + i * 2 + 1)
    in let c1 = peek-byte src (off + i * 2)
    in let p0 = poke-byte dst (i * 2) c0
    in let p1 = poke-byte dst (i * 2 + 1) c1
    in enum-ata-unswap src off dst (i + 1) words

  enum-ata-model : Integer -> Text
  enum-ata-model (buf) =
    let scratch = alloc-zeroed 64 8
    in let u = enum-ata-unswap buf 54 scratch 0 20
    in enum-ascii scratch 0 40

 Words 100-103 hold the LBA48 sector count and words 60-61 the LBA28
 count. A drive without LBA48 leaves the wide field zero, so nonzero
 wins and the narrow count is the fallback.

  enum-ata-sectors : Integer -> Integer
  enum-ata-sectors (buf) =
    let wide = peek-qword buf 200
    in if wide /= 0 then wide
    else fat16-read-u32 buf 120

Section: NVMe Identify Decoding

  enum-nvme-model : Integer -> Text
  enum-nvme-model (buf) = enum-ascii buf 24 40

Section: USB Inquiry Decoding

 INQUIRY answers a vendor of eight characters at byte 8 and a product
 of sixteen at byte 16; the readable name is both, separated when both
 are present.

  enum-usb-model : Integer -> Text
  enum-usb-model (inq) =
    let v = enum-ascii inq 8 8
    in let p = enum-ascii inq 16 16
    in if text-length v == 0 then p
    else if text-length p == 0 then v
    else v & " " & p

Section: FAT Volume Label

 The BPB carries the volume label at byte 43 on FAT16 and byte 71 on
 FAT32, eleven characters, space-padded.

  enum-fat-label : Integer, Boolean -> Text
  enum-fat-label (bpb) (is32) =
    enum-ascii bpb (if is32 then 71 else 43) 11

Section: ESP Probe

 With the selection cells steered at a candidate, one mount answers
 whether it carries a FAT ESP at all, one boot-sector read yields the
 label, and one root-directory lookup answers for CODEX.CDX.

  EnumEsp = record {
    ee-label : Text,
    ee-codex : Boolean
  }

  enum-no-esp : EnumEsp
  enum-no-esp = EnumEsp { ee-label = "", ee-codex = False }

  enum-esp : [Device.Port] EnumEsp
  enum-esp = act
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    if m.gm-ok == False then enum-no-esp
    else let vol = m.gm-vol
    in act
      b <- disk-read-sector (vol.vol-part-start)
      sz <- gfat-file-size vol "CODEX.CDX"
      let lbl = if b.dr-ok then enum-fat-label (b.dr-buf) (gfat-is32 vol) else ""
      in EnumEsp { ee-label = lbl, ee-codex = sz > 0 }
    end
  end

Section: Candidates

 Each candidate follows the same shape: prove the controller answers,
 steer the cells at it, probe its ESP, decode its identify data. The
 USB candidate exists only when usb-attach published a handle -- the
 connect fallback would reset the shared controller and kill the
 keyboard, the same rule GopMedium follows.

  enum-ahci : Integer -> [Device.Port] Maybe DiskInfo
  enum-ahci (n) = act
    hba <- ahci-find-nth n
    if hba.hba-ok == False then None
    else act
      let s = med-set med-kind-ahci n
      in act
        esp <- enum-esp
        let idbuf = alloc-zeroed 512 64
        in let ok = ahci-identify-on hba idbuf
        in let model = if ok /= 0 then enum-ata-model idbuf else ""
        in let secs = if ok /= 0 then enum-ata-sectors idbuf else 0
        in Just (DiskInfo { di-kind = med-kind-ahci, di-index = n, di-model = model, di-sectors = secs, di-label = esp.ee-label, di-codex = esp.ee-codex })
      end
    end
  end

  enum-nvme : [Device.Port] Maybe DiskInfo
  enum-nvme = act
    c <- nvme-disk
    if c.nc-ok == False then None
    else act
      let s = med-set med-kind-nvme 0
      in act
        esp <- enum-esp
        let idbuf = nvme-identify-ctrl c
        in let model = if idbuf /= 0 then enum-nvme-model idbuf else ""
        in Just (DiskInfo { di-kind = med-kind-nvme, di-index = 0, di-model = model, di-sectors = c.nc-sectors, di-label = esp.ee-label, di-codex = esp.ee-codex })
      end
    end
  end

  enum-ide : [Device.Port] Maybe DiskInfo
  enum-ide = act
    idbuf <- ide-identify
    if idbuf == 0 then None
    else act
      let s = med-set med-kind-ide 0
      in act
        esp <- enum-esp
        Just (DiskInfo { di-kind = med-kind-ide, di-index = 0, di-model = enum-ata-model idbuf, di-sectors = enum-ata-sectors idbuf, di-label = esp.ee-label, di-codex = esp.ee-codex })
      end
    end
  end

  enum-usb : [Device.Port] Maybe DiskInfo
  enum-usb = act
    let p = msc-published
    in if p.md-ok == False then None
    else act
      let s = med-set med-kind-usb 0
      in act
        esp <- enum-esp
        let inq = alloc-zeroed 64 8
        in let iok = msc-inquiry-into p inq
        in let model = if iok then enum-usb-model inq else ""
        in Just (DiskInfo { di-kind = med-kind-usb, di-index = 0, di-model = model, di-sectors = p.md-sectors, di-label = esp.ee-label, di-codex = esp.ee-codex })
      end
    end
  end

Section: Walk

 Save the selection cells, walk every candidate, restore. The restore
 writes the magic LAST for the same reason med-set does: a partial
 write must never read as a selection.

  enum-restore : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  enum-restore (magic) (kind) (index) =
    let k = poke-32 med-cells 4 kind
    in let i = poke-32 med-cells 8 index
    in poke-32 med-cells 0 magic

  enum-collect : Maybe DiskInfo, List DiskInfo -> List DiskInfo
  enum-collect (m) (acc) =
    when m
      is None -> acc
      is Just (d) -> list-push acc d

  enum-disks : [Device.Port] List DiskInfo
  enum-disks = act
    let m0 = peek-32 med-cells 0
    in let k0 = peek-32 med-cells 4
    in let i0 = peek-32 med-cells 8
    in act
      us <- enum-usb
      a0 <- enum-ahci 0
      a1 <- enum-ahci 1
      a2 <- enum-ahci 2
      a3 <- enum-ahci 3
      nv <- enum-nvme
      id <- enum-ide
      let r = enum-restore m0 k0 i0
      in enum-collect id (enum-collect nv (enum-collect a3 (enum-collect a2 (enum-collect a1 (enum-collect a0 (enum-collect us []))))))
    end
  end
